Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: a stripe whose walk failed after a walk rolling on an edge is
computed again with OCCT's equations in the surface/edge inverse function
(mitcad#1902, after 0170).

0170 makes the inverse function that ends a walk rolling on an edge where
the surface's contact reaches an arc (BRepBlend_SurfCurvConstRadInv) solve
the rolling ball's equations, so that the end section lies on the ball the
walk is. In OCCT's blend/complex A6 at scale 10 the walk on two edges that
starts from that end takes no step (each step too large down to the guide's
tolerance) and the rounding fails; with OCCT's equations there (both
contacts in the guide's normal plane) it is made at all five scales. Those
equations alone lose eight of MitCad's rounding tests, so they are only the
second solver:

- ChFi3d_Builder::Compute: a stripe that failed in the walk (a walking or
  start failure) and whose walks used the rolling inverse function is reset
  and computed again with OCCT's equations in it; it is kept where that
  succeeds, and stays failed as it was otherwise. The other stripes, and
  every stripe whose walk succeeds, are computed as before.
- ChFi3d_FilBuilder::PerformSurf (the two constant-radius surface/
  restriction walks): the inverse function's equations follow
  myRollingInverse, and each walk with the rolling ones is counted.
- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformSetOfSurf: myRollingInverse is false for the
  stripes computed again so (myOcctInverseStripes), also where a later step
  of Compute computes such a stripe again.

Upstream: with 0170 only; without it the inverse function is OCCT's already.

Failures (mitcad#1986): the second computation runs only for a stripe
that failed already; where it fails with OCCT's reports of a computation it
cannot make (ChFi3d_IsComputationFailure, 0016) the stripe stays bad and
the fillet fails as before. Any other failure (a broken invariant, a
signal) is rethrown.
Test: pending (mitcad#2003): the port test written for this patch does not
reach its case yet. Existing: geometry/tests/test_fillet_rolling_ball.cpp
(the rolling ball's cases).
